    "reasoning": "The target POST performs no rate-limit check before enableMfa or issueRecoveryCodes. After a user has a verified factor, repeated authenticated POST requests all pass the factor-state check, regardless of whether MFA was already enabled. issueRecoveryCodes synchronously generates and scrypt-hashes ten codes, then performs a delete followed by an insert through the service-role client, so each request consumes CPU and database capacity. Neither the route nor the helper serializes this delete/insert sequence or makes issuance idempotent. Concurrent requests can therefore repeatedly invalidate or interleave recovery-code sets, producing returned sets that may no longer correspond to the final persisted state. The same code-generation helper is also exposed by the recovery-codes route without a limiter, confirming there is no shared protection at this boundary. The recovery endpoint does use checkSessionRateLimit with ten attempts per minute, but that protection is not imported or invoked by either recovery-code issuance path. This is an authenticated, per-account availability and integrity issue rather than a cross-account data disclosure, so the reported MEDIUM severity is appropriate."
